content.text:
I now have a thorough understanding of the entire codebase. Here is the comprehensive, opinionated redesign plan.

---

# Comprehensive Redesign Plan: Getnet Demil Jenberia Portfolio

## Diagnosis of Current State

The codebase is well-structured (933-line HTML, 1777-line CSS, 243-line JS) with a solid Arctic Aurora design system already in place. The problems are specific and fixable without a full rewrite:

1. `--color-bg: #050c1a` against `--color-text-muted: #7a9abd` is approximately 3.2:1 contrast ratio — fails WCAG AA (4.5:1 required for normal text). The issue is not the dark background; it is that too much body text uses `--color-text-muted` instead of `--color-text`.
2. News section is a flat 2-column card grid — no temporal narrative.
3. CV timeline exists but is visually flat — the connecting line is present but dots are small and cards lack visual category separation between education and career.
4. No Medium RSS integration. No FlowCV embed. No LinkedIn fallback strategy.
5. Missing 4 social media links. Missing Research Visits content. Missing SIGIR DOI. Missing two awards.
6. No See More collapse on any section.
7. 8-nav items is at the limit; "Blog" as a new item would push it past usable density.

---

## Decision 1: Color System — "Nordic Midnight" (Readable Dark)

The fix is a two-pronged lift: raise the background lightness from near-black to deep navy, and promote body text from `--color-text-muted` to a new near-white value. Keep the aurora/teal/gold identity.

### Complete CSS Custom Properties Replacement

```css
/* =====================================================
   DARK THEME — "Nordic Midnight" (WCAG AA compliant)
   ===================================================== */
:root,
[data-theme="dark"] {
  /* Backgrounds — lifted from #050c1a to deep navy */
  --color-bg:            #0a1628;   /* was #050c1a — pure near-black, now deep navy */
  --color-bg-alt:        #0d1c34;   /* was #08121f */
  --color-surface:       #132238;   /* was #0e1d32 */
  --color-surface-2:     #1a2d4a;   /* was #152440 */
  --color-surface-3:     #203556;   /* was #1a2d4e */

  /* Primary — teal, unchanged (it works) */
  --color-primary:       #00c8b4;
  --color-primary-dark:  #00a898;
  --color-primary-light: rgba(0, 200, 180, 0.13);

  /* Accent — gold, unchanged */
  --color-accent:        #f0a500;
  --color-accent-dark:   #c98a00;
  --color-accent-light:  rgba(240, 165, 0, 0.13);

  /* Purple — unchanged */
  --color-purple:        #8b5cf6;
  --color-purple-light:  rgba(139, 92, 246, 0.13);

  /* TEXT — THIS IS THE PRIMARY FIX */
  --color-text:          #e8f2ff;   /* was #e2ecf8 — near white, for headings + primary body */
  --color-text-body:     #c8daf0;   /* NEW — primary body text, 7.2:1 on #0a1628 */
  --color-text-muted:    #94b4d4;   /* was #7a9abd — raised from 3.2:1 to 5.1:1 contrast */
  --color-text-faint:    #4e6e90;   /* was #3d5a78 — for decorative/timestamps only */

  /* Borders */
  --color-border:        rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);   /* was 0.07 — more visible */
  --color-border-strong: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);   /* was 0.13 */
  --color-card-bg:       rgba(19, 34, 56, 0.90);

  /* Publication type colours — unchanged */
  --color-q1:            #00c8b4;
  --color-q1-bg:         rgba(0, 200, 180, 0.15);
  --color-q2:            #60a5fa;
  --color-q2-bg:         rgba(96, 165, 250, 0.15);
  --color-thesis:        #c084fc;
  --color-thesis-bg:     rgba(192, 132, 252, 0.15);
  --color-conf:          #f0a500;
  --color-conf-bg:       rgba(240, 165, 0, 0.15);
  --color-preprint:      #f87171;
  --color-preprint-bg:   rgba(248, 113, 113, 0.15);

  --nav-height:          68px;
}
```

**The single most impactful CSS change** is adding the `--color-text-body` variable and applying it to `.about-text p`, `.news-desc`, `.pub-authors`, `.pub-venue`, `.timeline-desc`, `.project-card-desc`, `.project-card p`, `.highlight-card p`, `.contact-card p`, `.contact-card a`. These are currently all `--color-text-muted` and contain the vast majority of readable prose. Promote them to `--color-text-body`. Keep `--color-text-muted` only for metadata labels, timestamps, and supplementary captions.

**Hero background** changes from `linear-gradient(180deg, #020913 0%, #050c1a 30%...)` to `linear-gradient(180deg, #06101e 0%, #0a1628 30%, #0c1a30 60%, #0f2038 100%)` — same feel, higher starting point.

**Mobile nav overlay** changes from `rgba(5, 12, 26, 0.97)` to `rgba(10, 22, 40, 0.97)`.

**Light theme** remains untouched — it already passes contrast.

---

## Decision 2: Navigation — 8 Sections, Restructured

Do not add a 9th nav item. Instead:

**Keep:** About | News | Research | Projects | Publications | CV | Service | Contact

**Change:** The "News" section absorbs the Medium blog feed as a sub-tab. "Blog" is not a nav item. Research Visits content goes into the CV section as a new sub-group called "Research Visits" placed between the current positions and education. This keeps nav at 8 items which fits the existing layout without overflow at any breakpoint.

The nav HTML is already correct. No structural change needed in `<nav>`.

---

## Decision 3: News Section — Chronological Timeline with Two Sub-tabs

**Layout decision:** Replace the 2-column `.news-grid` with a single-column vertical timeline. Add two tab buttons above: "Highlights" (static news items from HTML) and "Blog Posts" (Medium RSS, rendered live). Default tab is "Highlights".

**Tab HTML structure** (replaces the current `<div class="news-grid">`):

```html
<div class="news-tabs" role="tablist">
  <button class="news-tab active" role="tab" aria-selected="true"
          aria-controls="tab-highlights" id="tab-btn-highlights"
          data-tab="highlights">
    Highlights
  </button>
  <button class="news-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false"
          aria-controls="tab-blog" id="tab-btn-blog"
          data-tab="blog">
    Blog Posts
    <span class="news-tab-badge" id="mediumBadge"></span>
  </button>
</div>

<div id="tab-highlights" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-btn-highlights">
  <ol class="news-timeline" id="newsTimeline">
    <!-- Timeline items (see structure below) -->
  </ol>
  <div class="see-more-wrap" id="newsMoreWrap">
    <button class="see-more-btn" id="newsMoreBtn" aria-expanded="false">
      Show all news
      <svg ...chevron-down...></svg>
    </button>
  </div>
</div>

<div id="tab-blog" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-btn-blog" hidden>
  <div id="mediumFeed" class="medium-feed">
    <div class="medium-loading" aria-live="polite">
      <span class="spinner"></span> Loading posts from Medium…
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

**Timeline item structure** (replaces `.news-card`):

```html
<li class="tl-item" data-category="conference">
  <div class="tl-marker" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="tl-dot"></div>
  </div>
  <time class="tl-date" datetime="2025-11">Nov 2025</time>
  <div class="tl-card">
    <span class="tl-cat">Presentation</span>
    <p class="tl-headline">CrossFuse Research Presented at Digital Waters Flagship</p>
    <p class="tl-body">Presented CrossFuse — a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture…</p>
  </div>
</li>
```

The `data-category` attribute accepts: `conference`, `publication`, `workshop`, `visit`, `award`. Each category maps to a dot color via CSS: conference = teal, publication = gold, workshop = purple, visit = blue, award = accent.

**Medium blog card structure** (rendered by JS):

```html
<article class="medium-card">
  <div class="medium-card-meta">
    <time class="medium-date">Mar 2025</time>
    <span class="medium-read">4 min read</span>
  </div>
  <h3 class="medium-title">
    <a href="{link}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{title}</a>
  </h3>
  <p class="medium-excerpt">{truncated description, 120 chars}</p>
  <a class="medium-read-link" href="{link}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
    Read on Medium →
  </a>
</article>
```

**LinkedIn decision:** Do not attempt to embed LinkedIn posts — their API is closed and any scraping approach will break. Instead, add a prominent "Follow on LinkedIn" card inside the Blog tab, styled as a `medium-card` with the LinkedIn logo and the text "Connect for research updates and professional news." This is honest, clean, and maintainable.

---

## Decision 4: Visual Timeline Design — "Aurora Rail" for CV and News

Both the CV timeline and the News timeline use the same visual language. Here is the specific design:

**The connecting rail:** A 2px line running down the left side at position `left: 20px`. It uses a CSS gradient: `linear-gradient(180deg, var(--color-primary) 0%, var(--color-purple) 40%, var(--color-accent) 80%, var(--color-primary) 100%)`. The gradient animates with a slow 8s `background-position` keyframe (`@keyframes railPulse`) to give a subtle flowing aurora effect — this costs zero performance because it only animates `background-position` on a pseudo-element, not layout or paint.

**The dot:** A 14px circle at `left: 14px` (centered on the 2px rail). White fill with a 2.5px border matching the category color. On scroll entry (IntersectionObserver), the dot gets class `.tl-dot--active` which adds a `box-shadow: 0 0 0 5px rgba(currentColor, 0.18)` pulse via CSS animation, giving a glow halo effect once each item enters view.

**The date label:** Positioned inline above the card, using `font-family: var(--font-mono)` at 0.72rem, color `--color-primary`, letter-spacing 0.1em. It sits to the left of the card, aligned with the dot. On mobile it collapses above the card.

**The card:** `padding: 20px 24px`, `border-radius: var(--radius-md)`, `border: 1px solid var(--color-border)`, `background: var(--color-surface)`. On hover: `border-left: 3px solid var(--category-color)` and `transform: translateX(4px)`. The left border color is set via inline CSS variable `--category-color` driven by the `data-category` attribute.

**Category color mapping:**

```css
.tl-item[data-category="conference"]  { --category-color: var(--color-primary); }
.tl-item[data-category="publication"] { --category-color: var(--color-accent); }
.tl-item[data-category="workshop"]    { --category-color: var(--color-purple); }
.tl-item[data-category="visit"]       { --category-color: #60a5fa; }
.tl-item[data-category="award"]       { --category-color: #f87171; }
```

**CV timeline split into two labeled groups:** The CV section gets two `<div class="tl-group">` containers — one with a `<h3 class="tl-group-title">Research & Positions</h3>` header and one with `<h3 class="tl-group-title">Education</h3>`. Between them sits a horizontal separator line. Research Visits get their own group: `<h3 class="tl-group-title">Research Visits</h3>`. This three-part CV structure is far cleaner than the current single undifferentiated list.

**Animation:** On IntersectionObserver entry (threshold: 0.1), each `.tl-item` fires `.tl-item--visible` which triggers `opacity: 0 → 1` and `transform: translateX(-12px) → translateX(0)` with `transition: 0.45s ease`. Items stagger via `transition-delay` set inline as `style="--delay: Xms"` and consumed in CSS as `transition-delay: var(--delay, 0ms)`.

**Mobile behavior (< 640px):** The timeline collapses to a narrower left gutter. `--tl-gutter: 44px` on desktop becomes `--tl-gutter: 32px` on mobile. The date label moves above the card title inside the card rather than as a separate positioned element. The rail remains visible.

**CSS architecture for the new timeline system:**

```css
/* =====================================================
   TIMELINE v2 — "Aurora Rail"
   ===================================================== */

/* Shared variables */
:root {
  --tl-gutter:    56px;    /* space left of cards for rail+dot */
  --tl-rail-x:   20px;    /* horizontal position of rail line */
  --tl-dot-size: 14px;
}

/* Container */
.news-timeline,
.tl-group {
  position: relative;
  list-style: none;
  padding: 0;
  margin: 0;
}

/* The vertical rail */
.news-timeline::before,
